 [
  {:id :community-fifteen-fr-prints-and-rulings :authority :printed-card-scans :edition :original
   :url "https://www.reedstonefood.com/agricola/compendium"
   :cards [:catalog/original-expansion-fr-26-grotto :catalog/original-expansion-fr-50-threshing-machine-plow :catalog/original-expansion-fr-60-wood-saw :catalog/original-expansion-fr-73-convict-number-24601 :catalog/original-expansion-fr-75-cowboy-and-mother :catalog/original-expansion-fr-83-good-friend :catalog/original-expansion-fr-94-miser :catalog/original-expansion-fr-114-turkey-breeder :catalog/original-expansion-fr-118-wood-gatherers :catalog/original-expansion-fr-119-workaholic]
   :artwork-manifest "resources/public/art/agricola/manifest.json"
   :notes "All ten final English prints were downloaded unmodified from PlayAgricola, visually checked, and individually recorded with source URL and SHA-256 in the artwork manifest. Grotto adds family capacity only after its five stored resources are gone. Threshing Machine Plow requires all three exact middle farm spaces and works on Farmland only. Wood Saw has no solo effect and is a card action, not Build Rooms. Convict blocks a distinct family placement starting the next round; feeding remains unchanged. Cowboy And Mother checks ordinary Work-phase action use and actual family including newborns. Good Friend uses actual paid building resources and includes majors played as minors. Miser discounts only an action-space build of exactly one room. Turkey Breeder's printed bonus-point symbol is an acknowledged printing error and is disregarded. Wood Gatherers includes eligible construction spending earlier in the round before it is played. Workaholic requires animals housed on the farm, not transient overflow."
   :interaction-review "Grotto and Good Friend reuse pictured resource choices with explicit costs, receipts, and visible remaining stock. Threshing Machine Plow uses the existing plow picker restricted to the exact middle spaces. Wood Saw opens direct farm room placement with normal graphic construction payments. Convict and Wood Gatherers resolve automatically and use impact history; prepared tables pause before an observable native action. Cowboy And Mother offers graphical food/animal rewards and opens direct farm placement for its field option. Miser reuses native room placement and payment previews. Turkey Breeder sequences direct farm-edge fencing and direct stable placement; its final debt is automatic. Workaholic shows each resource pile and its animal threshold, then uses graphical pile selection. Every card has a named prepared test table; native resolution, decline, persistence, replay and browser/mobile checks cover the reused flows."}
{:id :community-fifteen-wm :authority :printed-card-scans :edition :original
   :url "http://playagricola.com/Agricola/Cards/index.php"
   :cross-reference "https://www.reedstonefood.com/agricola/compendium"
   :note "WM006/009/019/033/047/056/068/081/105/107: final English actual card prints visually verified; local artwork manifest records source URLs and SHA256. Almshouse print costs one wood and one reed, correcting compendium transcription. Chemist and Storehouse Minder require three players. Family Planner arrivals count as Family Growth but Lady in Waiting explicitly does not. Begging provenance distinguishes family feeding and Borrower from other sources. Cash Crop points are permanent and only apply in the Harvest Field phase. Fish Pond excludes card-generated bonus food. Rotherham fields cannot touch diagonally or orthogonally. See docs/community-fifteen-wm-review.md for interactions and prepared tables."}
  {:id :community-fourteen-prints-and-rulings :authority :printed-card-scans :edition :original
   :url "http://playagricola.com/Agricola/Cards/index.php"
   :cross-reference "https://www.reedstonefood.com/agricola/compendium"
   :note "G001/017/053/057/064/070/077/090/103/108, WM005/013/032/039/053/064/103/104/110/114, FR020/025/066/081/085/086/091/093/106/110: all thirty final actual English prints visually inspected for identity, edition, costs, prerequisites and rules. Exact source URLs and SHA256 hashes are in resources/public/art/agricola/manifest.json. Paddocks requires one pasture and one occupation and passes left, correcting all three catalog omissions. Five Rings costs one wood OR one stone. Head Of The Revolution's print historically duplicates the FR085 corner label; its title and rules identify canonical FR086. Cowboy's physical print says its holding space remains unused, correcting the compendium's used transcription. Grain Mill Oven has no bread symbol and is not an oven for oven-upgrade prerequisites. Sheepdog and House Goat are mutually exclusive. Alternative Designer limits room substitutions per room and supports ordering with other cost changes; the explicit Original Frame Builder combo ruling allows wooden rooms for three wood and two reed (also reproduced in Agricola Norge 2024, p69), while Revised Frame Builder retains its printed once-per-room restriction. Storage Shed's stored stable remains built but off the farm and cannot be rebuilt. Mastermind counts repeated qualifying card plays and grey/negative bonus symbols; resources/agricola/bonus-symbol-cards.edn records visually checked supplemental symbols on cards whose scoring occurs through events rather than a :score definition. Interaction review: Alternative Designer and Stonepiler extend shared graphical construction payments, including mixed resources, per-room limits and stone rounding; Gimlet modifies the same cost preview automatically. Fish Seller uses an editable goods queue with pictured food prices capped by the Fishing pile actually received. Vegetarian modifies native graphical crop conversion and suppresses prohibited animal recipes. Bartering Hut combines an editable animal queue with explicit building-resource payment icons before passing the card. Corral, Sheepdog and Cowboy reuse direct farm animal housing with their distinct capacity/unused-space rules; Cowboy also reuses occupied action-space confirmation and Sheepdog appears in native feeding cost. Paddocks reuses direct fence-edge drawing restricted to internal pasture edges. Scythe extends graphical sowing to matching existing crop stacks, with each field selected at most once. Bee Keeper uses direct farm stable placement and a persistent sideways marker with no animal capacity. Hunter uses pictured conversions limited to the just-received animals. Pawnbroker uses owned improvement faces and an explicit printed-points-to-food exchange, then marks the improvement locked and pawned. Village Fool uses hand-card faces and persistent face-down stored cards. Bonfire and Raspberries share direct farm-marker placement, with distinct activation/automatic collection behavior; Bonfire activation exposes native cooking recipes for only that round. Reed Nursery adds reed icons to the existing direct-field sowing flow and a graphical endgame exchange from actual supply/field pieces. Storage Shed uses direct stable removal followed by the shared improvement payment preview with a prepayment resource choice. Wine Closet uses the actual future round track and a pictured vegetable-to-food/points delivery choice. Five Rings uses graphical alternative-cost selection with a matching persistent resource marker. Grain Mill Oven uses major faces in minor actions and explicit discard-for-oven payment followed by optional native baking. Boatswain uses direct empty physical-field selection and does not emit sow events. Fiddler and Manual Labourer use automatic end-round resource receipts and visible card impact history. Harvester and Head Of The Revolution use automatic native crop/farm event tracking and shared scoring; their prepared tables pause before the qualifying action. Mastermind uses automatic card-play tracking, including majors, without extra popups. Sailboat Constructor uses visible remaining resource stacks and an editable ordered purchase with cumulative food prices. Stroller uses six ordered resource icons with direct move controls, including all four building-resource types, and visible harvest deliveries. Every card has a fresh native prepared review table; resolution, optional decline, persistence, replay and desktop/mobile review checks accompany the batch."}
  {:id :community-thirteen-prints-and-rulings :authority :printed-card-scans :edition :original
   :url "http://playagricola.com/Agricola/Cards/index.php"
   :cross-reference "https://www.reedstonefood.com/agricola/compendium"
   :note "G002/006/047/056/065/071/079/094/101/104, WM021/034/045/049/058/062/069/087/099/113, FR002/005/014/046/062/065/067/074/092/097: all thirty final English prints visually inspected for identity, costs, prerequisites and rules. Exact image source URLs and SHA256 checksums are in resources/public/art/agricola/manifest.json. Artisan and Seasonal Plants use final v2 faces; FR014 is printed Chocolade while its canonical catalog ID retains Chocolate. Printed Country Doctor requires three planted fields, not the compendium transcription's grain-only restriction. Printed Straw-Thatched Hut requires two plowed fields, not planted fields. Hammer follows the printed passing arrow and compendium missing-pass-text erratum. Fence Dealer follows the erratum retaining all supply pieces but limiting built fences to twelve. Animal Welfarist excludes Renovation plus Fences and card-generated stables/fences; Artists Garden is a real extra field harvest. Backyard Well cannot receive Water Carrier, Flagon or Well Builder benefits, but can upgrade to Village Well. Interaction review: Animal Farmer reuses visible housing and live scoring, including stable capacities; Cow Bell reuses direct graphical animal housing on unused farm spaces. Artisan and Potter's Wheel reuse the graphical native workshop feeding exchanges with automatic receipt bonuses. Understudy reuses normal action-space confirmation with an occupied-space permission and no separate decision. Beehive Oven reuses baking recipes with explicit oven-use choice where it changes food yield. Resource Collector adds an editable resource-icon order tied to future round markers. Hammer and Fence Dealer reuse direct farm-edge fencing and graphic cost previews; Hammer also displays payment to fenced opponents. Remodeling and Changing Plans reuse direct stable selection followed by direct room or field placement, preserving passing continuation. Seasonal Plants highlights only fields actually sown in the triggering action. Livestock Keeper uses a pictured food-to-extra-offspring choice followed by normal graphical housing. Stable Deliveryman reuses actual round-track reservation and direct farm stable arrival with one-wood payment. Sunrise Admirer highlights only legal eastern-column spaces with a two-food price and an explicit decline/discard. Apple Tree and Backyard Well use direct farm placement and persistent wood/stone markers, with future food markers for the well. Gold Medal uses graphical scoring-category choices. Pig Wallow uses direct empty-field removal followed by direct single-cell pasture placement. Wild Breeding highlights only action piles that received an animal, then reuses native housing. Absinthe uses direct action-board food placement at Work start. Artists Garden uses direct planted-field/card-field harvest selection. Chocolade reuses occupation payment with visible card food stock and no generic resource selector. Straw-Thatched Hut uses the normal room placement flow and pictured alternative room price. Animal Welfarist and Parquet Setter use automatic Returning Home receipts and card impact history without extra confirmation. Benefactor combines graphical animal payment and direct free-room placement. Cabbage Lover uses a pictured clay-to-stored-point exchange bounded by converted vegetables. Country Doctor reuses native Family Growth with its once-per-game no-room permission. Martial Artist uses minor-card faces with the two-food reward per discarded card. Each card has a named native prepared review table in eurozone.card-scenarios; automated resolution, decline, persistence and recording checks and browser/mobile flow checks accompany the batch."}
  {:id :community-eleven-prints-and-rulings :authority :printed-card-scans :edition :original
   :url "http://playagricola.com/Agricola/Cards/index.php"
   :cross-reference "https://www.reedstonefood.com/agricola/compendium"
   :note "G003/004/023/032/048/060/083/084/098/115, WM008/017/027/030/037/050/071/072/084/089, FR019/024/031/033/036/052/078/090/098/105: final English prints visually verified against identity, printed cost, prerequisites and text. Exact source URLs and SHA256 checksums are recorded in resources/public/art/agricola/manifest.json. Apprentice, Wintercrafter, Holiday Present and Pumpkin Pie use final v2 faces rather than obsolete prototypes. G115 Fish Farm retains the source's historical gminortouristfarm.jpg filename. FR036 is titled March on its final print, while its canonical catalog identity retains Marsh. Heiress follows the compendium erratum removing the before-phase-one parenthetical. Pumpkin Pie's alternate cost is two grain or one vegetable; Holiday Present costs one wood or one clay. Interaction review: Animal Holder reuses normal occupation play and native global tie-aware scoring. Apprentice uses an automatic receipt at the harvest boundary with no redundant popup. Glutton uses six pictured food-to-point recipes after normal final feeding. Hobbyist, Ringmaster and Wintercrafter reuse the improvement-face picker with separate minor/major entry payments shown graphically. Holiday Present reuses the pictured animal/begging choice and native passing-card continuation. Horse Barn modifies native sow stacks beside actual stable pieces, without a new decision. Pumpkin Pie reuses the baking flow and an optional pictured crop-to-points confirmation. Fish Farm adds a direct farm/card-field crop picker, allowing one selected grain or vegetable per field without emitting harvest events. Bread Carrier reuses automatic future-round food markers. Engineer adds its receipt before native improvement payment and previews the resulting affordability. Heiress reuses direct farm room placement, graphical room costs, and material-specific round markers. Hoarder uses pictured building-resource storage choices with persistent card markers and scoring. Master Stableman reuses direct farm stable placement, now before animal collection, with one-wood prices and a two-stable maximum. Tapestry Weaver and Cookies use a shared new direct-room scoring-food flow; markers remain attached to their chosen rooms and are separate per card. Crib uses a pictured gain-food/pay-food-for-point choice at work-phase end. Friendly Pastures uses native graphical animal housing with a two-species limit only in stabled pastures. Home Garden uses native renovation receipts and visible future food markers, including same-round play. Evening Prayer reuses sequential occupation-face choices with pictured one-food fees. Golden Rose modifies the native food payment preview without a separate choice and preserves extra occupation costs specified by a card. Heatwave reuses major-improvement faces, free Fireplace costs and explicit return-Fireplace upgrade choices. Kids Corner reuses graphical house-pet housing with independent species slots. March uses pictured different-animal pairs followed by direct selection of exactly two farm fields; payment remains atomic with placement and can be declined. Trees for the Citizens reuses native cost previews and printed-cost scoring. Drawing Genius reuses the optional improvement-face picker after the newest round action. Lemon Trader uses graphical grain/vegetable exchange recipes with a shared twice-per-round limit. Pasteurization Expert reuses the visible resource stack and simultaneous-receipt ordering flow, excluding breeding. Reformer uses native graphical housing on the actual played occupation cards. All thirty have native-triggered prepared review tables with Previous/Restart/Next navigation; resolution, optional decline, saved state and recording playback are covered by eurozone.card-scenarios-test. Rule tests: agricola.community-batch-eleven-test and agricola.community-eleven-engine-test. Desktop and mobile graphical flows are exercised by browser_community_batch_eleven.py."}
  {:id :community-ten-prints-and-rulings :authority :printed-card-scans :edition :original
   :url "http://playagricola.com/Agricola/Cards/index.php"
   :cross-reference "https://www.reedstonefood.com/agricola/compendium"
   :note "G018/022/024/026/028/031/055/058/059/102, WM002/010/031/044/046/052/063/083/085/091, FR004/041/049/056/063/072/077/089/109/117: final English prints visually verified with exact URLs and SHA256 in the art manifest. Helper uses final v2, not the prototype. Truffle Digger requires wood on an accumulation space. Watering Can is a passing card; Apple Garden replaces the catalogue's Apple Orchard title; Dove Hunter replaces the catalogue's erroneous Chandler text. Interaction review: Fisherman's Apprentice uses the occupation-face picker with food paid to the triggering player. Glass Blower, Dove Hunter and Peasants Boutique use graphical once-per-harvest conversion recipes. Grafter, Grain Farmer, Truffle Digger, Wheedler and Stage Star apply automatic native receipts at their printed timing. Grump, Village Farmer, Humble Farmboy and Tile Maker use native play previews, immediate goods and global tie-aware scoring. Helper shares native occupied construction spaces including five-player linked Build Room/Traveling Players, never own workers or emptied accumulation spaces. Sausage Grinder modifies native cooking rates and fixed animal recipes, never enables cooking by itself. Animal Buyer uses nine pictured one/two-animal purchases and native housing; both animals must be funded before cooking either purchase, as clarified by the compendium. Cloister Dweller and Apple Garden use native scoring, without extra decisions. Silo Girl shows parity-specific future-round markers. Stone Curator uses native stone receipts and printed-cost scoring. Birdcage has a pictured food-to-stored-point choice and forbids house pets. Free-Range Chickens has a new graphical partial-collection quantity control; uncollected food retains its scoring value. Fruitcake discounts each person including newborns only at the next feeding. Iron Plow uses direct farm cells followed by optional graphical sowing, once only. The Port Le Havre uses a pictured clay-to-stone choice after baking. Watering Can grows all planted grain/vegetable stacks including card fields, ignores wood and empty fields, then passes. Art Director and Wealthiest European use the editable building-resource queue. Cocotte uses the improvement-face picker with an atomic additional major fee shown graphically. Landscape Gardener uses two directly clickable card fields with grain/vegetable sowing; no numbered field selector. All thirty have native prepared review tables and persistence/replay verification. Tests: agricola.community-batch-ten-test."}
  {:id :community-nine-prints-and-rulings :authority :printed-card-scans :edition :original
   :url "http://playagricola.com/Agricola/Cards/index.php"
   :cross-reference "https://www.reedstonefood.com/agricola/compendium"
   :note "G100/109/111/114/116, WM065/066/086/094/115, FR021/035/079/087/116: all final English prints verified; Tree House prototype rejected for final v2. Exact source URLs and SHA256 hashes are in the art manifest. Sheepskin Rug accepts Simple Fireplace but not Cooking Hearth; Sickle counts planted crops but only physical field tiles. Stonecutter's Hall has three alternative costs. Glassblowers Shop returns Pottery, remains dual major/minor, and suppresses a repurchased Pottery's bonus but not its conversion. Flat Hill's planted fields include harvestable wood stacks. Interaction review: Reed Sale, Sheepskin Rug, Flat Hill and Drinker of Absinthe reuse direct card-play previews and automatic native receipts/renovation/round markers. Sickle and Boundary Stones reuse direct farm plowing with live card scoring. Tree House and Lighthouse reuse native growth confirmation; no room tile is added. Stonecutter's Hall, Brazier and Glassblowers Shop reuse graphical exchange recipes, restricted to one unit once per feeding phase, including while other players are still feeding; no redundant quantity input or farm. Lamp Oil funds occupations before payment; Village Druid rewards later occupations automatically. Worm Bait uses visible stored food, automatic harvest removal and Fishing collection. Immigrants Son uses the optional direct-farm plow popup for the fifth through seventh occupations, excluding itself. All fifteen have native prepared tables and save/replay checks. Tests: agricola.community-batch-nine-test."}
  {:id :community-eight-prints-and-rulings :authority :printed-card-scans :edition :original
   :url "http://playagricola.com/Agricola/Cards/index.php"
   :cross-reference "https://www.reedstonefood.com/agricola/compendium"
   :note "G013/016, WM001/059, FR012/015: final English faces visually verified; exact source URLs and hashes in artwork manifest. Clay Starter charges its extra food only for a two-room wooden hut and emits a native renovation. Fence Helper responds to other players building fences, including card effects, pays that player, and retains normal fencing costs. Amazon rewards only Fishing in rounds 7/9/11/13 with sheep/boar/cattle/chosen animal. Apprenticeship compares occupations with every other player, keeps additional occupation costs, and passes left. Camembert places food on three distinct revealed spaces, including occupied ones; the next user collects it. Coffee Break cancels only its own future deliveries when its owner plays an occupation. Interaction review: graphical animal/occupation choices, direct farm fence edges with payment previews, direct shared-board food placement with editable selections, and automatic renovation/future markers. All six have native-triggered review tables. Tests: agricola.community-batch-eight-test."}
  {:id :community-seven-prints-and-rulings :authority :printed-card-scans :edition :original
   :url "http://playagricola.com/Agricola/Cards/index.php"
   :cross-reference "https://www.reedstonefood.com/agricola/compendium"
   :note "G078/095, WM078/088, FR011/088: final English faces verified against identity, costs, prerequisites, and rules; Private Market's outdated three-food prototype was rejected for the final two-food v2 print. Source URLs and checksums are in the artwork manifest. Grain Market exchanges once after a grain harvest, including Manure and Harvest Helper, never a grain receipt or crop movement. Farm School counts actual harvested stacks including wood and preserved vegetables, only in a real Harvest. Grain Elevator caps stock at player count and opponents clear it to the supply without receiving it. Brickyard collects only at four or more clay, remains active, and only refills when supply clay is zero. Journeyman requires a stone house and offers a graphical optional food-for-animal choice. Native card-face occupation selection includes a payment preview; exchanges and collect-all abilities use existing graphical flows. All six have native-triggered review tables; Grain Elevator also has an opponent table. Tests: agricola.community-batch-seven-test."}
  {:id :community-six-prints-and-rulings :authority :printed-card-scans :edition :original
   :url "http://playagricola.com/Agricola/Cards/index.php"
   :cross-reference "https://www.reedstonefood.com/agricola/compendium"
   :note "G012/097, WM022/096, FR071/096: final English prints checked visually; exact source URLs and checksums in artwork manifest. Carrot Farmer sells three vegetables only after Farmland; Food Connoisseur automatically removes the top marker only for food taken with a person from its three named spaces. Oceanographer rewards one field per plow action, not a multi-field plow. Pulley keeps normal room costs and discounts and receives Day Laborer goods before payment. Oak Cask stores food outside personal supply, grows only in real harvests while nonempty, and may be emptied at any time without discarding the card. Child Care Worker reacts to other players' family growth. Interaction review: stock markers and graphical payment, direct farm room selection and payment preview, graphical exchange choices, and a collect-all card ability. All six have native-triggered review tables. Tests: agricola.community-batch-six-test."}
  {:id :community-five-prints-and-rulings :authority :printed-card-scans :edition :original
   :url "http://playagricola.com/Agricola/Cards/index.php"
   :cross-reference "https://www.reedstonefood.com/agricola/compendium"
   :note "G107/119, WM077/079, FR001/101. Verified final English faces, costs, prerequisites and rules; G archive prototypes were rejected in favor of final v2 faces. Exact URLs and checksums are in the artwork manifest. Sheep Market uses graphical optional harvest exchanges before fields; Festival Hall exchanges after fields, not Manure harvests. Edible Roots observes the last good on the entire field, including card fields and mixed stacks, not taking/moving crops. Abandoned Willow requires an empty farm field whose removal preserves connectivity, selected directly on the farm. Wooden Bridge rewards only its two action spaces; Powerhouse checks supply after round preparation, at work start; when an available anytime ability can precede that bonus, its owner gets a preparation window (Politician combination has a separate test table). Each has a native-triggered review table with Previous/Restart/Next navigation. Tests: agricola.community-batch-five-test."}
